    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099415": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Below the Bar) in Management, Cork University Business School",
      "description": "Closing Date: 17/11/2026 Lecturer (Below the Bar) in Management, Cork University Business School Specific Purpose Whole-Time Post (anticipated duration 30th June 2029) UCC wishes to appoint an academic to the role of Lecturer (Below the Bar) in Management. Reporting to the Head of Department, the appointable candidate will demonstrate a track record of relevant research and teaching experience. We are especially interested in receiving applications amongst those with expertise in the International Business, Entrepreneurship, Innovation and/or Strategy domains, who are committed to a programme of intellectually rigorous and relevant scholarship that is publishable in top journals and creating wider impact, while also being able to deliver high-quality, engaged teaching across undergraduate and postgraduate levels. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to Professor Lawernce Dooley, Email: L.Dooley@ucc.ie For further information on the Department www.cubsucc.com. University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives. Appointment may be made on the Lecturer (Below the Bar) Salary Scale: €43,028 - €73,909 (Scale B) / €45,116 - €70,327 (Scale A). Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate **.** Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to recruitment@ucc.ie, quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Tuesday, 17th November 2026 No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector Contact Person : Professor Lawrence Dooley Contact Email : l.dooley@ucc.ie Job ID : 099415 Contact Number : Close Date : 17-Nov-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099953": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Researcher - School of Public Health",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Post-Doctoral Researcher - School of Public Health 24 Months Fixed-Term, Whole-Time Post We are recruiting a Post-Doctoral Researcher with experience in conducting evidence syntheses, qualitative research, knowledge translation, co-design and/or stakeholder engagement to join the Integrating perinatal Mental health supports in Primary Care (IMPaCT) project. The IMPaCT project aims to develop practical approaches to better include supports for perinatal mental health in primary care. The Post-Doctoral Researcher will be responsible for leading a rapid review, conduct of qualitative research with primary care professionals, and leading stakeholder engagement to co-develop practical solutions and ways of delivering perinatal mental health care and training in primary care. They will work with the multi-disciplinary and trans-sectoral team, and other project staff, to achieve the project aims under the direction of Dr Karen Matvienko-Sikar. They will also be responsible for writing up, disseminating, and translating project findings. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. Project - Integrating perinatal Mental health supports in Primary Care For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/ The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal inquiries can be made in confidence to Dr Karen Matvienko-Sikar, Senior Lecturer, School of Public Health, Tel: + 353 (0) 21 4205512; Email: Karen.msikar@ucc.ie For further information on the hiring unit, please see: https://www.ucc.ie/en/publichealth/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives Salary: €47,273 - €53,925 p.a (IUA PD1 Research Salary Scale) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 30th October 2026. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector. Contact Person : Dr. Karen Matvienko-Sikar Contact Email : Karen.msikar@ucc.ie Job ID : 099953 Contact Number : +353 (0) 21 4205512 Close Date : 30-Oct-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099913": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Researcher in Early Modern History - School of History and Radical Humanities Laboratory",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/11/2026 Post-Doctoral Researcher in Early Modern History - School of History and Radical Humanities Laboratory 36 Months Fixed Term, Whole-time Post Applications are invited for the position of Post-Doctoral Researcher in Early Modern History on the DeepCattle project, a 5-year ERC Consolidator Grant led by Prof Eugene Costello in the School of History and Radical Humanities Laboratory, University College Cork. DeepCattle breaks new ground in global environmental history by investigating the pre-industrial origins of commercial cattle farming. It uncovers how the growth of beef and dairy production in rural areas of Europe fed trade networks and the rise of urban centres over the period, c.1240-1840 AD. At the same time, it reveals the consequences of increasingly market-focused cattle farming for society and biodiversity in rural regions. Its main study areas are in Ireland, Scotland and Sweden, with secondary study areas in the Alps and Hungary. DeepCattle is a highly interdisciplinary project, combining research in historical archives with archaeological and palaeoecological fieldwork. We are seeking a Post-Doctoral Researcher in Early Modern History to join our interdisciplinary team for 3 years. The successful candidate will work with us to examine changes in farming practices, landholding and trade associated with the commercialisation of cattle farming, with a focus on early modern Scotland and Ireland (c.1450-1700). This is a period in which administrative records about property, production and commerce start to become plentiful for livestock-focused areas of Europe. Many of these records remain under-exploited, however. The Post-Doctoral Researcher will undertake research on manuscript sources from Scotland and Ireland in order to uncover evidence for the growth of commercial cattle droving and dairying in these countries, and understand how this development transformed rural society and the wider economy. Moreover, by joining DeepCattle, the successful candidate will have the opportunity to expand their international network and develop an interdisciplinary profile. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. Project : DeepCattle: The Deep History of Commercial Cattle Farming in Europe, c.1240-1840 AD For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/ The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal inquiries can be made in confidence to Eugene Costello, Professor, School of History and Radical Humanities Laboratory; Email: ecostello@ucc.ie For further information on the hiring unit, please see: https://www.ucc.ie/en/history/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives Salary: €47,273 - €53,925 p.a (IUA PD1 Salary Scale) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 20th November 2026. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector. Contact Person : Prof. Eugene Costello Contact Email : ecostello@ucc.ie Job ID : 099913 Contact Number : Close Date : 20-Nov-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099813": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Palaeoecology - Department of Archaeology & the Radical Humanities Laboratory",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/11/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher in Palaeoecology - Department of Archaeology & the Radical Humanities Laboratory 36 Months Fixed-Term, Whole-Time Post Applications are invited for the position of Post-Doctoral Researcher in Palaeoecology on the DeepCattle project, a major new 5-year project funded by an ERC Consolidator Grant. DeepCattle will break new ground in global environmental history by tracing the origins of commercial cattle farming. It investigates how the growth of beef and dairy production in rural areas of Europe fed trade and the rise of cities. At the same time, it will investigate the consequences of increasingly commercial cattle production for society and biodiversity in rural landscapes. DeepCattle is a highly interdisciplinary project, combining historical and archaeological methods with palaeoecology (palynology and sedimentary ancient DNA). The project is led by Prof Eugene Costello (UCC) and involves collaboration with Dr Benjamin Gearey (UCC) and Dr Peter Heintzman (Centre for Palaeogenetics, Stockholm University). We are seeking a Post-Doctoral Researcher in Palaeoecology to join our interdisciplinary team for three years and work with us to trace the expanding environmental footprint of cattle farming since the Middle Ages. The Post-Doctoral Researcher will assist in lake and peatland coring in Ireland, Scotland and Sweden and be responsible for palynological analysis of the cores at UCC. In collaboration with the Centre for Palaeogenetics (CPG) in Stockholm University, the Post-Doctoral Researcher will also undertake sedimentary ancient DNA analysis on selected cores. The Post-Doctoral Researcher will have expertise in palaeoecology, including palynology, and will be interested in working with the team to integrate palaeoecological data with historical and archaeological evidence. The Post-Doctoral Researcher will also be motivated by the opportunity to develop skills in sedimentary ancient DNA at a world-class facility in CPG Stockholm. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. Project - DeepCattle: The Deep History of Commercial Cattle Farming in Europe. For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/ The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal inquiries can be made in confidence to Prof. Eugene Costello; Email: ecostello@ucc.ie For further information on the hiring unit, please see: https://www.ucc.ie/en/radicalhumanitieslaboratory/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives Salary: €47,273 - €53,925 p.a (IUA PD1 Research Salary Scale) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 20th November 2026. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector. Contact Person : Prof. Eugene Costello Contact Email : ecostello@ucc.ie Job ID : 099813 Contact Number : Close Date : 20-Nov-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099893": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher School of Biochemistry & Cell Biology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 13/11/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher School of Biochemistry & Cell Biology 30 Months Fixed-Term, Wholetime Post The successful candidate will join the TRIBeCA project, an innovative research programme investigating how dysregulation of the endosomal recycling pathway contributes to glioblastoma invasion and resistance to therapy, with the ultimate goal of identifying novel biomarkers and therapeutic targets. Working as part of a multidisciplinary team, the postholder will develop and apply advanced 3D human cellular models that recapitulate glioblastoma infiltration of healthy brain tissue. Applicants with expertise in the culture and differentiation of human induced pluripotent stem cell (iPSC)-derived models, protein biochemistry, molecular and cellular biology, and high-resolution confocal imaging are particularly encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will lead two project work packages, contribute to experimental design and data analysis, and play an active role in the supervision and mentorship of undergraduate and postgraduate researchers. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. Project - “Tracking Recycling in Brain Cancer: The TRIBeCA Study” For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/ The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal inquiries can be made in confidence to to Dr. Andrew Lindsay (Principal Investigator), School of Biochemistry & Cell Biology, Tel: + 353 (0) 21 4901380; Email: a.lindsay@ucc.ie For further information on the hiring unit, please see:https://www.ucc.ie/en/biochemistry/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives Salary: €47,273 - €53,925 p.a (IUA PD1 Research Salary Scale) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 13th November 2026. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector. Contact Person : Dr. Andrew Lindsay Contact Email : a.lindsay@ucc.ie Job ID : 099893 Contact Number : + 353 (0) 21 4901380 Close Date : 13-Nov-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099773": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral School of Mathematical Sciences",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/11/2026 Postdoctoral School of Mathematical Sciences 24 Months Fixed-Term, Whole-Time Post We invite applications for a postdoctoral position in the field of Applied Mathematics (Network Dynamics, Multiscale Methods, Critical Transitions). The postdoc will be part of a cross-disciplinary research team including members of the Applied Mathematics Group of the School of Mathematical Sciences and the School of Biological, Earth and Environmental Sciences at UCC. The postdoc will be based in the UCC School of Mathematical Sciences. The aim of the project is to develop new mathematical techniques for the analysis of nonlinear phenomena in dynamical networks. These phenomena include tipping points and adaptation across multiple timescales, with applications in restoration ecology. Ecosystems are an example of such networks. These new techniques will advance the fields of adaptive network dynamics and critical transitions, providing a basis for modelling real ecosystems, particularly that of the Lough Hyne Marine Nature Reserve in West Cork. In addition, the successful applicants will have the unique opportunity to work in an interdisciplinary team at UCC and abroad, participate in field trips to Lough Hyne, develop predictive and data-driven theoretical models for real ecosystems, and contribute to the design of novel restoration protocols and conservation measures. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. Project - Adaptive dynamical network solutions to ecological restoration For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/ The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal inquiries can be made in confidence to Dr. Serhiy Yanchuk, Dr, Department of Applied Mathematics, Tel: + 353 (0) 21 4205818; Email: syanchuk@ucc.ie or Dr. Sebastian Wieczorek, Professor, Head of Applied Mathematics, Tel: + 353 (0) 21 4205828; Email: sebastian.wieczorek@ucc.ie For further information on the hiring unit, please see: https://www.ucc.ie/en/matsci/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives Salary: €47,273 - €53,925 P.A. (IUA PD1 Research Salary Scale) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 27th November 2026. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector. Contact Person : Dr. Serhiy Yanchuk Contact Email : syanchuk@ucc.ie Job ID : 099773 Contact Number : Close Date : 27-Nov-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=098694": {
      "title": "Professional Practice Placement Manager (Early Years and Childhood Studies) School of Education",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Professional Practice Placement Manager (Early Years and Childhood Studies) School of Education Permanent Whole-Time Post UCC wishes to appoint a practitioner to the role of Professional Practice Placement Manager (Early Years and Childhood Studies). Reporting to the School Manager, the successful applicant will provide support to the B.A (EYCS) by managing and delivering a quality placement experience for the students on the programme. The appointee will be responsible and accountable for the day-to-day management and administration of the student placement element (c. 300 students) of the programme in co-operation with 100+ partner settings. The appointee will also be responsible for leading and developing the team of EYCS professional practice placement supervisors. The appointee will collaborate with colleagues across the three Schools, University Offices, as well as external bodies. The successful candidate will have a relevant Postgraduate Qualification and a qualification in Early Years and Childhood Studies or a related area. They will also have extensive experience of working with children in early years settings. The candidate will be required to demonstrate excellent leadership, organisational, interpersonal and IT skills as well as a proven ability to work independently and as a member of a team. They should also demonstrate the energy, initiative and enthusiasm that are requirements of this post. Given the nature of the post, a full clean driving licence and the use of a car are essential. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to Dr Tara Singleton, School Manager, by Email: tara.singleton@ucc.ie For further information on the Department http://www.ucc.ie/en/education/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives . Appointment may be made on the Admin III (Grade 6) Salary Scale: €61,471 - €73,392 (Scale B) / €58,529 - €69,839 (Scale A). Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate. Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to recruitment@ucc.ie, quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Tuesday, 10th November 2026 No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector Contact Person : Dr. Tara Singleton Contact Email : tara.singleton@ucc.ie Job ID : 098694 Contact Number : Close Date : 10-Nov-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099753": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - Cancer Research @UCC",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Research Assistant - Cancer Research @UCC 24 Months Fixed-Term, Whole-Time Post The UCC Cancer Trials Group (UCC CTG) is looking for an enthusiastic Research Assistant/ Translational Research Coordinator to support cancer microbiome studies at the Mercy University Hospital, as part of Precision Oncology Ireland work at Cancer Research@UCC. About UCC CTG: The UCC CTG is an HRB funded collaborative team which aims to bring together a dedicated team of cancer specialists and research staff across the south of Ireland. The UCC CTG is part of Cancer Research @UCC, a translational cancer research centre with the designation of UCC Futures Strategic Research Centre within the College of Medicine & Health at University College Cork. The UCC CTG group includes 3 cancer trials units at Cork University Hospital (Cancer Trials Cork @CUH), University Hospital Waterford and Bons Secours Hospital Cork). For more information about the group, check our website: https://ucccancertrials.ie or our X @UCCCancerTrials About the Clinical Research Facility Cork : The facility was established to promote excellence in the design, safe conduct and analysis of patient-focused research. ‎ It provides the necessary space, expertise and equipment to allow medical professionals in Munster to work at the very cutting edge of clinical research. The HRB CRF-UCC supports research undertaken through UCC and affiliated hospitals. It is operated as a joint venture with UCC, CUH and MUH. The post holder will be supporting Precision Oncology Ireland clinical studies at the Mercy University Hospital, as part of the CRF team, and will also be part of the POI team at Cancer Research@UCC. https://www.precisiononcology.ie ; https://www.ucc.ie/en/cancer-research/research/microbes-and-cancer/ The Research Assistant/Clinical Trials Associate is required to manage the workflow of patient sample and data collection and processing. This role requires excellent communication and interpersonal skills, computer literacy, and data management. He/she is a key member of the project team and should be able to prioritise and work in line with project management decisions. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. Project - Precision Oncology Ireland For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal inquiries can be made in confidence to Mark Tangney, Email: m.tangney@ucc.ie For further information on the hiring unit, please see: https://www.ucc.ie/en/cancer-research/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives Salary: €34,129 - €44,311 p.a (IUA RA Salary Scale) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 23rd October 2026. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector. Contact Person : Mark Tangney Contact Email : M.Tangney@ucc.ie Job ID : 099753 Contact Number : Close Date : 23-Oct-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099973": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - School of Public Health",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Research Assistant - School of Public Health 7 Months Fixed-Term, Part-Time Post (0.5 FTE) We are recruiting an enthusiastic and organised Research Assistant with strong communication skills to join the Integrating perinatal Mental health supports in Primary Care (IMPaCT) project. You will be responsible for supporting conduct of a rapid review and preparation of qualitative and stakeholder engagement activity materials under the direction of Dr Karen Matvienko-Sikar and project staff. The IMPaCT project aims to develop practical approaches to better include supports for perinatal mental health in primary care. The project will also explore what training healthcare professionals need to provide these supports. To achieve this, we are undertaking a rapid review, qualitative research with healthcare professionals, and stakeholder engagement to co-develop practical solutions and ways of delivering perinatal mental health care and training in primary care. Project - Integrating perinatal Mental health supports in Primary Care For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/ The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal inquiries can be made in confidence to Dr Karen Matvienko-Sikar, Senior Lecturer, School of Public Health, Tel: + 353 (0) 21 4205512; Email: Karen.msikar@ucc.ie For further information on the hiring unit, please see: https://www.ucc.ie/en/publichealth/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives Salary: 50% of €34,129 - €44,311 p.a (IUA Research Assistant Scale) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 30th October 2026. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector. Contact Person : Dr Karen Matvienko-Sikar Contact Email : Karen.msikar@ucc.ie Job ID : 099973 Contact Number : + 353 (0) 21 4205512 Close Date : 30-Oct-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099933": {
      "title": "Research Assistant Cancer Research @ UCC",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Research Assistant Cancer Research @ UCC 12 Months Fixed-Term, Whole-Time Post The post holder will contribute to the data management portfolio of studies taking place in the Cancer Trials Cork Unit at the Cork University Hospital. This role requires excellent communication and interpersonal skills, computer literacy, organisation and an awareness of time management. The Research Assistant is required to manage data in the department of Cancer Trials Cork at CUH. He/she is responsible for the creation, updating, maintenance and validation of clinical study databases, and for the provision of computerised reports of these data. He/she is a key member of the clinical project team and should be able to prioritise and work in line with project management decisions. The responsibilities of the Research Assistant are to manage clinical trials through review, computerisation, cleaning and auditing of clinical data and databases in compliance with standard operating procedures and regulatory agency guidelines. The role of Research Assistant is to ensure the data is accurate and consistent before it is passed on to the study team for analysis which will determine the outcome of the trial. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. Project - Cancer Trials Cork @CUH For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal inquiries can be made in confidence to Ali Noureldein, Data Management Lead, Cancer Trials Cork, Tel: + 353 (0) 21 423 4805, Email: AliNoureldin@ucc.ie For further information on the hiring unit, please see: https://www.ucc.ie/en/cancer-research/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives Salary: €34,129 - €44,311 p.a (IUA RA Salary Scale) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 23rd October 2026. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector. Contact Person : Ali Noureldein Contact Email : AliNoureldin@ucc.ie Job ID : 099933 Contact Number : + 353 (0) 21 423 4805 Close Date : 23-Oct-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099873": {
      "title": "Research Fellow - Sustainability Institute",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Research Fellow - Sustainability Institute 12 Months, Fixed Term, Whole-time Post The successful candidate will join the Coastal and Marine Research (CMR) Group in the Sustainability Institute and will be based in the Institute’s Beaufort Building in Ringaskiddy. A key aim of the CMR Group and the Sustainability Institute’s is to connect research and innovation to society. This funding from the European Centre for Medium-Range Weather Forecasting (ECMWF) is focused on Copernicus Climate Change Service (C3S) and the Copernicus Atmosphere Monitoring Service (CAMS). The Research Fellow will play a key role in delivering UCC's contributions to C3S datasets and products, while also leading the design, development and delivery of training activities across a range of disciplinary and professional contexts. Working closely with a multinational consortium and stakeholders across Europe and overseas, the postholder will contribute to the implementation and development of best practice in capacity development, curriculum design and training delivery, while advancing research into effective approaches for vocational, professional and technical education. An important component of the role will also involve independent quality review of Copernicus datasets and products, providing expert assessment of scientific quality, uncertainty, methodological soundness and user relevance to support the continued development of the Copernicus portfolio. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. Project Title: ECMWF - Copernicus Climate Change and Atmospheric Monitoring Services For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/ The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to Jeremy Gault, Co-ordinator, CMR Group, Sustainability Institute, Email: j.gault@ucc.ie For further information on the https://www.ucc.ie/en/sustainability-institute/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives. Appointment may be made on the IUA Scale for Research Fellow: €67,214 - €73,239 p.a. Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal https://ore.ucc.ie/ Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday 23rd October, 2026 No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector Contact Person : Jeremy Gault Contact Email : J.Gault@ucc.ie Job ID : 099873 Contact Number : Close Date : 23-Oct-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099993": {
      "title": "Senior Postdoctoral Researcher School of Nursing and Midwifery",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Senior Postdoctoral Researcher School of Nursing and Midwifery 24 months, Fixed-Term, Whole-Time Post Funded by Horizon Europe under the EU Cancer Mission, TRANSCEND-XR is an innovative project aiming to co-create and evaluate a digital health intervention using Extended Reality (XR) to support adolescent and young adult testicular cancer survivors across Europe. The TRANSCEND-XR intervention will improve awareness of late effects, address unmet supportive care needs, and enhance quality of life. Co-developed with survivors, care partners, and healthcare professionals, TRANSCEND-XR will be evidence-based, ethically grounded, and scalable in real-world settings. More information on the project can be found here: https://cordis.europa.eu/project/id/101214413 The Senior Post-Doctoral Researcher will serve as the Project Manager for the TRANSCEND-XR project. In this capacity, they will be primarily responsible for overseeing and executing the project in close collaboration with the Principal Investigator/Project Coordinator and research teams spanning 12 European countries. Their role will encompass comprehensive project management duties, as well as active involvement in the scientific aspects of the project, including data collection, analysis, and data management. The Senior Post-Doctoral Researcher will have postdoctoral/postgraduate research experience as well as experience in the management and administration of multi-stakeholder research projects with a working knowledge of healthcare research (preferably focused on cancer) and/or research funding agencies such as Horizon Europe, Health Research Board, and/or Research Ireland, among others. Project - TRANSCEND-XR: Co-creation and implementation of an intervention to improve the understanding of TesticulaR cANcer late effects and unmet Supportive CarE NeeDs of AYA survivors using eXtended Reality For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/ The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to Mohamad Saab, Professor of Nursing and TRANSCEND-XR Project Coordinator, School of Nursing and Midwifery, Tel: + 353 (0) 21 490 1467; Email: msaab@ucc.ie For further information on the Department: https://www.ucc.ie/en/nursingmidwifery/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives. Salary: €55,399 - €60,250 p.a. (IUA PD2 Research Salary Scale) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate. Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal https://ore.ucc.ie/ Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 23rd October, 2026. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector Contact Person : Professor Mohamad Saab Contact Email : msaab@ucc.ie Job ID : 099993 Contact Number : + 353 (0) 21 490 1467 Close Date : 23-Oct-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=098384": {
      "title": "Student Health Doctor (0.75FTE), Student Health Department",
      "description": "Closing Date: 17/11/2026 Student Health Doctor (0.75FTE), Student Health Department Permanent Part-Time Post (0.75FTE) UCC wishes to appoint a dedicated doctor working 7.5 sessions per week at the Student Health Department. Reporting to the Head of Student Health the successful candidate will provide medical care to registered students at University College Cork as part of a primary-care multidisciplinary team dedicated to promoting student wellbeing and helping students thrive. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to Dr Judy Dwyer, Tel: 021-4902311, Email: j.dwyer@ucc.ie For further information on the Department https://www.ucc.ie/en/studenthealth/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives . Appointment may be made on the Student Health Doctor Salary Scale: €236.82 (pro-rata) ) 3 hour sessions for 7.5 sessions per week. Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate . Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to recruitment@ucc.ie, quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Tuesday, 17th November 2026 No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector Contact Person : Judy Dwyer Contact Email : J.Dwyer@ucc.ie Job ID : 098384 Contact Number : Close Date : 17-Nov-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=097460": {
      "title": "Student Health Nurse, Student Health Department",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Student Health Nurse, Student Health Department Permanent Part-Time Post (0.42FTE) for 36 weeks UCC wishes to appoint a Nurse to the Student Health Department in UCC. Reporting to the Head of the Student Health Department the successful candidate will join our experienced team of doctors, nurses, physiotherapists and administration staff. We are seeking a nurse with a team orientated approach and ability to work in a busy environment. The successful candidate will play a key role in delivering high quality care, health promotion and support to UCC students attending the medical centre. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to Nurse Claire O’Callaghan, Tel: 021 4902311 Email: cocallaghan@ucc.ie For further information on the Department https://www.ucc.ie/en/studenthealth/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives . Appointment may be made on the Clinical Nurse Manager II Salary Scale: €62,699 - €79,227 (pro-rata) for 36 weeks. Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate. Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to recruitment@ucc.ie, quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Tuesday, 10th November 2026 No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector Contact Person : Ms. Claire O’Callaghan Contact Email : cocallaghan@ucc.ie Job ID : 097460 Contact Number : Close Date : 10-Nov-2026 12:00 "
