    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=098993": {
      "title": "Academic Administrator, School of Computer Science and Information Technology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 24/11/2026 Academic Administrator, School of Computer Science and Information Technology Permanent Whole Time Post UCC wishes to appoint an administrator in the School of Computer Science and Information Technology responsible for the academic administration of postgraduate taught and research programmes and contribute to the academic administration of undergraduate programmes, as well as general school admin tasks that may arise from time to time, as appropriate. Reporting to the School Manager, the appointee will be accountable for taught postgraduate programmes, including student recruitment and admission support, administration of programme changes, governance, coordination of examinations, external examiner reviews, and timetabling management. In addition, the administrator will be responsible for the administration of the PhD lifecycle (approx. 70 students), from registration to graduation, with particular oversight of the PhD review process in conjunction with the new UCC Doctoral Institute. The administrator is expected to drive digital process improvements using tools such as SharePoint, power automate, and Power BI. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to the Head of School, Professor Dirk Pesch, Email: dirk.pesch@ucc.ie For further information on the School: https://www.ucc.ie/en/compsci/. University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives . Appointment may be made on the Admin V Salary Scale: €52,338 - €64,190 (Scale B) / €50,066 - €61,097 (Scale A). Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate . Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to recruitment@ucc.ie, quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Tuesday, 24th November 2026 No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector Contact Person : Professor Dirk Pesch Contact Email : dirk.pesch@ucc.ie Job ID : 098993 Contact Number : Close Date : 24-Nov-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=098388": {
      "title": "IT Analyst (Research Computing), IT Services",
      "description": "Closing Date: 17/11/2026 IT Analyst (Research Computing), IT Services Fixed Term Whole-Time Post (duration 3 years) UCC wishes to appoint to the role of IT Analyst (Research Computing). Reporting to the Research Computing Manager or nominee, the appointable candidate will support the delivery, operation, and development of University College Cork's Research Computing services. The post-holder will work closely with researchers, research centres, IT Services colleagues, and external partners to support High Performance Computing (HPC), research data storage platforms, cloud-based services, and associated research infrastructure. The role combines technical operations, user support, service administration, documentation, onboarding, and stakeholder engagement. The successful candidate will help ensure researchers can access reliable, secure, and scalable computing and data services to support world-class research activities across the University. This aligns with the University's growing investment in research computing, HPC platforms, research data storage, and cloud-enabled research services. IT Services provides essential services to the University community and plays an ever more critical role in the strategic development of University College Cork. IT Services is responsible for leading Digital Services and Digital Transformation at University College Cork. The department manages one of the largest private networks in Ireland, 2 datacentres, over 7000 computer workstations, 300+ websites, research infrastructure and a customer base of more than 30,000 users. In addition, it provides and supports the University’s business systems and Teaching and Learning technologies, along with a large portfolio of staff and student online services. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to Jerry Deasy, IT Services Research Computing Manager, Email: j.deasy@ucc.ie For further information on the Department https://www.ucc.ie/en/it/. University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives. Appointment may be made on the Admin V (Grade 5) Salary Scale: €52,338 - €64,190 (Scale B) / €50,066 - €61,097 (Scale A). Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate . Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to recruitment@ucc.ie, quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Tuesday, 17th November 2026 No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector Contact Person : Jerry Deasy Contact Email : j.deasy@ucc.ie Job ID : 098388 Contact Number : Close Date : 17-Nov-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=097765": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Above the Bar) In Integrated Childrens’ and General Nursing, School Of Nursing and Midwifery, College of Medicine and Health",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/12/2026 Lecturer (Above the Bar) In Integrated Childrens’ and General Nursing, School Of Nursing and Midwifery, College of Medicine and Health Permanent Whole Time Post UCC wishes to appoint to the role of Lecturer in Integrated Children’s and General Nursing. The appointee will be expected to contribute to teaching, assessment and programme development and administration at undergraduate and postgraduate level, as well as engage in relevant research. The successful appointee will hold a professional children’s and general qualification, have held or hold a registration (or be eligible to register) in Children’s and General Nursing with the Nursing and Midwifery Board of Ireland. Applicants will be expected to have an academic qualification to Master’s level and be registered for or have completed a PhD / Doctorate. Applicants also expected to have considerable children/paediatric nursing experience. The School of Nursing and Midwifery was established in 1994 and since then a wide range of programmes have been developed at both undergraduate, postgraduate and doctorate levels. In addition, the School has a growing suite of Continuing Professional Development modules supporting life-long learning. Since 2012, the School has actively engaged in international recruitment and now has a growing body of international students. The School now has over 1,400 students and is one of the largest in the University. The School is committed to excellence and leadership in teaching, practice and research involving close collaborative relationships with health service providers, and national and international educators and researchers. The School holds the Athena Swan Bronze Award and is in the top 30 in QS subject Rankings for Nursing. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to Professor Patricia Leahy-Warren, Tel: 021-4901462/1553, Email: headofsonm@ucc.ie Further information on the School of Nursing & Midwifery please visit http://www.ucc.ie/en/nursingmidwifery University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives. Appointment may be made on Lecturer (Above Bar) Salary Scale: €80,776 - €104,610 (Scale B) / €76,852 - €99,378 (Scale A). Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate . Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to recruitment@ucc.ie, quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Tuesday, 1st December 2026 No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector Contact Person : Professor Patricia Leahy-Warren Contact Email : headofsonm@ucc.ie Job ID : 097765 Contact Number : Close Date : 01-Dec-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=098695": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Below the Bar) in Music Composition (Experimental Music & Sound Art), Department of Music, School of Film Music and Theatre",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/01/2027 Lecturer (Below the Bar) in Music Composition (Experimental Music & Sound Art), Department of Music, School of Film Music and Theatre Permanent Whole Time Post UCC wishes to appoint an academic to the role of Lecturer (Below the Bar) in Music Composition (Experimental Music & Sound Art). Reporting to the Head of Department. The Department of Music invites applications from innovative practitioners working in diverse sound-based arts. The outstanding candidate will have a demonstrated record of success in both academic and arts-professional fields in creative practice involving experimental music and improvisation, and will preferably have extensive experience with other forms of art involving sound, such as sound art, installation art, multimedia and/or performance art. The candidate must be able to teach courses involving music technology to Masters level, and will ideally be highly conversant with a range of technologies for live interactive performance, including programming with MaxMSP. Applicants should also be able to offer music composition courses at undergraduate level that are in line with the unique philosophical outlook of UCC’s Music Department and its approach to the study of music in general. Joining the Programme Director, Dr. Jeffrey Weeter, the ideal candidate will become part of a close-knit team delivering the Department’s highly successful and unique MA Experimental Sound Practice. Applicants should address their suitability to the specific demands of this MA in any application. The candidate will be expected to maintain a busy schedule of research outputs at the highest levels of excellence and to supervise PhDs in innovative creative practices across diverse musical and artistic genres. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to Dr Jeffrey Weeter Tel: +353 021 490 4530, Email: j.weeter@ucc.ie For further information on the Department https://www.ucc.ie/en/fmt/music/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives . Appointment may be made on the Lecturer (Below the Bar) Salary Scale:€43,028 - €73,909 (Scale B) / €45,116 - €70,327 (Scale A) . Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate . Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to recruitment@ucc.ie, quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Tuesday, 12th January 2027. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector Contact Person : Dr Jeffrey Weeter Contact Email : j.weeter@ucc.ie Job ID : 098695 Contact Number : Close Date : 12-Jan-2027 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=100074": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher School of Applied Social Studies",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/11/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher School of Applied Social Studies 19 Months Fixed-Term, Whole-Time Post The School of Applied Social Studies at UCC has a reputation for inclusive, participatory based, research with adults with intellectual disability (id+ Project | University College Cork). The position advertised here will build on this expertise. Inclusive Co-Learning and Inclusive Research with Students with Intellectual Disability in Higher Education The PATH 4 Phase 2 funded id+ futures Project delivers 2 rights-based, inclusive education programmes for people with intellectual disabilities (ID) at UCC which: • promote and embed a rights-based approach to third-level education for people with ID based on the human rights principles of the United Nations Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities. • provide university-based co-learning opportunities for students with and without ID • contribute to development of capacity in inclusive teaching, learning and assessment across the Higher and Further Education sectors • develop inclusive research resources to support people with ID to engage as ‘experts by experience’ in academic research Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. Project - id+ Futures Project For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/ The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal inquiries can be made in confidence to Dr. Nicola Maxwell, Programme Director id+ Futures Project, School of Applied Social Studies; Email: n.maxwell@ucc.ie For further information on the hiring unit, please see: https://www.ucc.ie/en/appsoc/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives Salary: €47,273 - €53,925 p.a (IUA PD1 Research Salary Scale) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 20th November 2026 No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector. Contact Person : Dr. Nicola Maxwell Contact Email : n.maxwell@ucc.ie Job ID : 100074 Contact Number : Close Date : 20-Nov-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=100073": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - School of Law",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Research Assistant - School of Law 4.5 Months Fixed-Term, Part-Time Post The PI is looking to recruit a graduate of the BCL International programme to consider how the formulaic assessments of Irish law degrees can be updated to maximise student critical learning and discipline dexterity. The Research Assistant will use their firsthand experience of their time spent on Erasmus, to reflect on how civil law approaches to law assessment can provide an alternative approach which improves learning outcomes, building stronger confidence, and disciplinary identity alongside a richer, more critical understanding of the law. Together with the PI, the Research Assistant will design a “LawMax Toolkit” which will identify best practice legal pedagogies and help Irish legal educators disrupt the gamification of legal degrees, encouraging deeper critical engagement and maximising learning autonomy and identity. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. Project - LawMax For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal inquiries can be made in confidence to Dr Samantha Morgan-Williams, Director, Centre for Criminal Justice & Human Rights, School of Law, UCC Tel: + 353 (0) 21 490 2699; Email: samantha.williams@ucc.ie For further information on the hiring unit, please see: https://www.ucc.ie/en/ccjhr/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives Salary: (0.38 FTE) €34,129 - €44,311 p.a (IUA RA Salary Scale) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 30th October 2026. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector. Contact Person : Dr. Samantha Morgan-Williams Contact Email : samantha.williams@ucc.ie Job ID : 100073 Contact Number : + 353 (0) 21 490 2699 Close Date : 30-Oct-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=100033": {
      "title": "Research Assistant School of Nursing and Midwifery",
      "description": "Closing Date: 13/11/2026 Research Assistant School of Nursing and Midwifery 6 Months Fixed-Term, Whole-Time Post The School of Nursing & Midwifery is currently recruiting a Research Assistant to work on the In-Touch Horizon Europe funded project. We are seeking a highly motivated researcher to join an international study evaluating the In-Touch intervention. In-Touch is a person-centred palliative care programme for people with advanced dementia in nursing homes. This ambitious 5-year project is being led by UCC, and the project involves a pan-European cluster randomised controlled trial in 56 nursing homes across 7 European countries. The successful applicant will work within in the School of Nursing & Midwifery, under the direction of the Principal Investigator, Professor Nicola Cornally. The successful candidate will contribute to administration of the project, and dissemination of findings. More information about the project can be found at the following website https://palliativeprojects.eu/in-touch/. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. Project - In-Touch: Implementation of a person-centered palliative care iNtervention To imprOve comfort, QUality of Life and social engagement of people with advanced dementia in Care Homes For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal inquiries can be made in confidence to Noeleen Brady, Senior Research Coordinator, School of Nursing and Midwifery, UCC on behalf of Professor Nicola Cornally, Tel: + 353 (0) 21 490 1568; Email: noeleen.brady@ucc.ie For further information on the hiring unit, please see: https://www.ucc.ie/en/nursingmidwifery/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives Salary: €34,129 - €44,311 p.a (IUA RA Salary Scale) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 13th November 2026. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector. Contact Person : Noeleen Brady Contact Email : noeleen.brady@ucc.ie Job ID : 100033 Contact Number : + 353 (0) 21 490 1568 Close Date : 13-Nov-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=100013": {
      "title": "Research Assistant Technical - Biological Services Unit",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Research Assistant Technical - Biological Services Unit 6 Months Fixed-Term, Whole-Time Post This position, funded by APC Microbiome Ireland, supports the operation, maintenance and regulatory compliance of the Biological Services Unit (BSU) at University College Cork. The postholder will provide technical and operational support to research programmes under the direction of the Manager and Deputy Manager. The role involves conducting a range of laboratory and animal facility duties, including data collection and record management, preparation laboratory materials, operation and maintenance of equipment and supporting approved research procedures. The postholder will contribute to the efficient running of the laboratory and animal research facilities while ensuring compliance with established protocols and project requirements. The role includes responsibility for maintaining high standards of animal care and welfare through the provision of daily husbandry, welfare monitoring and participation in weekend and out-of-hours rotas as required. The postholder will maintain laboratory and animal facility inventories, ensure equipment is properly maintained and available, support training and practical demonstrations and uphold rigorous standards of health, safety, hygiene, and record keeping. The successful candidate will possess knowledge of regulatory requirements governing preclinical animal research, including Scientific Animal Protection legislation and Directive 2010/63/EU. Experience working within a Specific Pathogen Free (SPF) or high-containment animal facility, together with qualifications or relevant experience in laboratory animal science, animal management, or animal care and welfare, will enable the postholder to support research activities while ensuring compliance with ethical, scientific and welfare standards. UCC is an accredited research organisation and as such can form hosting agreements with third country nationals (and non-EEA nationals) for the purpose of conducting research in the University. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. Project : APC funded support in the BSU For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to the BSU Manager at BSUManager@ucc.ie For further information on the hiring unit, please see: https://www.ucc.ie/en/bsu/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives Salary: €34,129 - €44,311 p.a (IUA RA Salary Scale) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 30th October 2026. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector. Contact Person : BSU Contact Email : BSUManager@ucc.ie Job ID : 100013 Contact Number : Close Date : 30-Oct-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=100053": {
      "title": "Research Fellow - School of Physics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Research Fellow - School of Physics 12 Months, Fixed Term, Whole-time Post The ideal candidate will have a combination of specialized knowledge, skills and successful experience in several of the following research areas: Sub-Kelvin Refrigeration Concepts, operation and maintenance of sub-kelvin 3He-refrigerators and 3He-4He dilution refrigerators, based on both liquid 4He and cryofree cryostats. Ultra-low Vibration Techniques Concepts, operation and maintenance of ultra-low vibration experiments, including control of mechanical, acoustic, cabling, vacuum-line, and cryogenic noise. High-field Superconducting Magnets Concepts, operation and maintenance of persistent superconducting magnets at fields up to 20 Tesla. Superconducting Quantum Inference Devices & Qubits Concepts and operation of Josephson junctions, SQUIDS and superconducting qubits. High-frequency Data Acquisition and Control Electronics Concepts, operation and maintenance GHz bandwidth, high signal/noise data acquisition and storage. Instrument Design and Fabrication Principles of, and experience with, scientific instrument design; project management, CAD tools, prototyping, machining, assembly, integration, testing and operation of custom built instruments. Spectroscopic Imaging Scanning Tunneling Microscopy Concepts, operation and maintenance of low temperature spectroscopic imaging STM including, quasiparticle interference imaging of correlated metals, topological states and superconductors. Quantum Matter Theory Knowledge and experience of contemporary theory for topological superconductors, Bose condensates, topological heavy fermions, ferromagnetic topological insulators, Dirac and Weyl semimetals, magnetic monopole fluids, quantum spin liquids and exciton fluids. Data Management, Analysis & Machine Learning Knowledge and experience in high volume image-array data acquisition and management, development of custom software and machine learning techniques for advanced image-array data analysis. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. Project Title: Macroscopic Quantum Science and Technology - MARQUIS For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/ The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to J.C. Séamus Davis, Professor of Quantum Physics, School of Physics, Tel: + 353 (0) 21 490 2482; Email: jcseamusdavis@gmail.com For further information on the https://www.ucc.ie/en/physics/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives. Appointment may be made on the IUA Scale for Research Fellow: €67,214 - €73,239 p.a. Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal https://ore.ucc.ie/ Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday 30th October, 2026 No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector Contact Person : Prof. J.C. Seamus Davis Contact Email : jcseamusdavis@gmail.com Job ID : 100053 Contact Number : + 353 (0) 21 490 2482 Close Date : 30-Oct-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=098726": {
      "title": "Technical Assistant, Biological Services Unit",
      "description": "Closing Date: 24/11/2026 Technical Assistant, Biological Services Unit Fixed Term Whole-Time Post (duration 12 months) The Biological Services Unit (BSU) at University College Cork is a leading research facility committed to advancing scientific discovery through the ethical and responsible use of animals in research. We are currently seeking to appoint an experienced professional to the role of Technical Assistant within the facilities. Reporting to BSU Management, the Technical Assistant will play a crucial role in animal welfare and husbandry, legislative compliance, research support and service provision. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to BSU Manager, Email: BSUManager@ucc.ie For further information on the Department https://www.ucc.ie/en/bsu/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives . Appointment may be made on the Technical Assistant Salary Scale: €32,422 - €45,041 (Scale B) / €34,133 - €43,384 (Scale A). Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate . Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to recruitment@ucc.ie, quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Tuesday, 24th November 2026 No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector Contact Person : Kieran McManamon Contact Email : k.mcmanamon@ucc.ie Job ID : 098726 Contact Number : Close Date : 24-Nov-2026 12:00 "
    }
  },
